  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nThe north wind is starting to have that \"feels like snow\" vibe \nto it today. Air temperatures across the region are generally \nsitting in the upper 30s to low 40s this afternoon, with the \nKMPX and KDLH radars only showing a few light returns of \nsprinkles or flurries. Our forecast pattern is centered around \nthe progression of the high pressure over the central Plains. \nNortherly winds become light and variable as the center of the \nhigh shifts south over Minnesota and Wisconsin. This will \nsupport at least a couple more days of similar conditions to today,\nbut with even cooler mornings thanks to clearing skies \novernight. That said, the cold air advection cloud cover is \nstill quite dense over western Wisconsin and southeast \nMinnesota. It can be difficult to fully erode stratus decks in \nthese types of air masses, and forecast soundings suggest a \nshallow layer of saturated profiles will likely linger through \nmuch of the night in these spots. Expect temperatures to run a \nfew degrees warmer in areas where cloud cover persists \novernight. \n\nThere is greater confidence for clear skies Saturday morning as\nthe high is almost directly over the Upper MS by this point. \nFor most people, this will be the coldest morning, with \nwidespread lows near 20 degrees. This is about 5-8 degrees below\nnormal for lows in mid-November, and nearly 30 degrees below \nour average temperature for the month so far. It is not an \nextreme cold by any means, but it will certainly feel colder \nthan what we've been spoiled with so far this fall.\n\nThe cold stretch is short-lived as the high pressure begins to\nmove over the Ohio River Valley during the start of next week,\nplacing us under southerly flow on the back side of the high. \nWe expect afternoon highs to climb back into the 50s for most \nareas, and overnight lows to settle into the 40s. The \ncombination of the warm lows and mild highs will have average \ntemperatures running 15-20 degrees above normal for next week. \nEnsemble and deterministic guidance show overwhelmingly dry \nconditions over the next 7 days, with the best chance for any \nprecipitation not coming until next Thursday as a shortwave \ntrough kicks northeast out of the Rockies.",
